    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420765663/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """talked about Nietzsche - whose value, according to him, is that he consistently hates all that makes against life and loves all that makes for it. His concrete admirations are constantly changing, but his criteria of excellence - Wertschätzungen - remain astonishingly the same. His  Geburt der Tragödie  is the best thinking on aesthetics from our point of view, the really psychological - that Bernhard knows. 
Mr. Warner came in at tea-time and told us about the Mexican cliff dwellers and other out-of-the-way, interesting things. 
Saturday May 18. ‘95 
An earthquake at 8.53 p.m. lasting 5 seconds. We were at dinner at the Palmerino. """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 137 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1894-95> .

